no, just skin would form there.

00:01:54.331 --> 00:01:56.820
123Music, "If I scratch my mosquito bite,

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:57.690
will it get itchier?"

00:01:57.690 --> 00:01:58.710
Absolutely.

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:01.530
The reason this happens is
because of histamine production,

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.540
the more you scratch,
the more inflammation,

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:05.100
the more histamine, the bigger the hive,

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:06.480
the more itchy it becomes.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:08.610
And if you cause excoriations,

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:10.260
which means openings in the skin,

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.660
you could actually create
an infection on top of it,

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:13.493
which is a problem,

00:02:13.493 --> 00:02:15.030
'cause now you're gonna
have to put steroidal cream

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:16.080
to decrease the itching,

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:18.367
and then an antibiotic cream
to fight the infection.

00:02:18.367 --> 00:02:21.060
"Is it true that if you
chew gum while studying,

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.460
then chew the same flavor
gum while taking the test,

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:25.710
you'll recall the information
you studied better?"

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:26.790
It's possible.

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:29.190
They say that you should try and replicate

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:31.410
your study environment as much as you can

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:32.880
during your testing environment.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.160
I think that probably goes a step too far.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.020
You're probably spending more effort

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:40.020
in trying to do this
kind of hacking thing,

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:41.250
than actually the effort

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:42.960
that you could be spending on studying.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:45.360
If you love chewing gum and
you love the minty flavors,

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:47.220
stick with the minty flavor, don't change.

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:49.110
And know that if you cram
and you stay up all night,

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:50.880
it won't enter your long term memory,

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.057
the information you studied.

00:02:52.057 --> 00:02:54.540
"Is it weird to go to a
doctor for multiple things

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:55.380
at the same time?

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:57.180
What should I do if I have
several things to go over

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:58.013
and only few minutes?"

00:02:58.013 --> 00:02:58.846
Great question.

00:02:58.846 --> 00:03:00.810
Can we cover multiple issues in one visit?

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:02.850
Sometimes yes, sometimes no.

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:05.190
If your symptoms are connected,

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:08.100
and can be pointing to
one universal problem

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:09.630
that's causing all of these symptoms.

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:12.630
If you have diarrhea,
are sweating profusely,

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:14.010
are having palpitations,

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:16.080
perhaps you're having a thyroid issue.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.720
And had you only presented
one of those concerns to me,

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:20.250
I might miss the thyroid issue,

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:21.600
'cause I think, "Oh, diarrhea,

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.180
maybe just an upset stomach
related to something you ate."

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:25.350
Why sometimes no?

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:27.750
Well, if you come in and
you say your foot hurts

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:29.190
because you sprained your ankle,

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:30.987
but then you also get headaches sometimes,

00:03:30.987 --> 00:03:33.720
and then you're also
due for your pap smear.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.240
You could see how we can't get
all that done in one visit,

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:39.120
but a good doctor will say
what's the most important to you,

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:40.860
and what they think the most important

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:42.810
is to address in a given moment,

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:44.310
meaning what's the most timely,

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:46.507
and then we can reschedule
you for a follow up visit.

00:03:46.507 --> 00:03:47.970
"Does it matter when during the day

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:49.410
humans get eight hours of sleep?

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:51.090
I have friends who are bartenders at night

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:52.110
and then sleep during the day."

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:53.880
If you're getting seven
to nine hours of sleep,

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:55.050
that's important,

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:57.270
but then consistency is also important,

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:59.490
and then also the time of day
of sleep is also important.

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:01.410
Humans are not nocturnal creatures,

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:03.030
so we need to be out in the sun,

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:04.860
and that's how our circadian rhythms work.

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:08.940
People who work during the
night and sleep during the day

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:11.100
have more health problems on average.

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.590
And we don't have the exact
reason as to why that happens,

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:15.000
but we know that it's definitely present.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.400
We actually even have a disorder
called shift work disorder

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:19.027
from individuals who have that problem.

00:04:19.027 --> 00:04:20.310
"When I have aches and pains,

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:21.930
I treat it with a cocktail approach,

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:24.480
some acetaminophen, some
ibuprofen, some aspirin,

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:25.350
all at once.

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:26.610
Should I continue this approach,

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:28.080
or is one better than the other?

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.150
Is one better than the
other is actually a question

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:31.440
I get a lot from patients.

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.750
They're like, "I have this
ache or I have a fever."

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:37.050
All three of these medications
are pain and fever reducers.

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:37.950
There's certain instances

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:39.900
where I would recommend
one over the other,

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:41.160
one is not the best.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:43.140
For example, if you have kidney problems,

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:46.170
you may not wanna be taking
an ibuprofen medication

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:47.850
which gets excreted through the kidneys

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:49.650
and can potentially damage the kidneys.

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:52.140
If on the other hand, you
have really bad liver disease,

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:54.570
maybe you wanna not be
taking acetaminophen,

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:56.970
'cause that gets metabolized
within your liver.

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:58.950
There's different instances
where you wanna be smart,

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:00.690
for example, if you're prone to bleeding

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:01.830
and have a bleeding disorder,

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:04.410
aspirin can actually
inactivate your platelets

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:05.640
and cause you to bleed more.

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:08.760
I try and minimize the
use of these medications,

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:11.490
because reducing fevers is
not always a good thing.

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:13.140
A fever is beneficial,

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:16.170
it's helping you overcome
whatever infection you may have,

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:18.180
so unless there's a reason,

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:21.337
we shouldn't be lowering
a fever in many cases.

00:05:21.337 --> 00:05:23.866
"Has a patient ever come in
with a disease or a condition

00:05:23.866 --> 00:05:25.710
you've never heard of before?"

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:26.670
Absolutely.

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:28.290
Remember I'm a family medicine doctor,

00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:31.170
anything can walk through
my door at any time.

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:34.290
And I have to not only figure that out,

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:36.840
but also maybe even learn
about that condition

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:37.860
right there on the spot.

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:39.600
And the more open you
can be with a patient

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:41.820
about what you know and
what you don't know,

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:43.680
the better that line of
communication's gonna be,

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:46.597
because not everyone can know
all the answers right away.

00:05:46.597 --> 00:05:49.410
"Why does taking a long, deep
breath help calm you down?"

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:52.050
Because it actually
activates your vagus nerve.

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:53.820
That is the primary nerve in your body

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:55.950
responsible for parasympathetic tone,

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:59.880
meaning your ability to begin
rest, repair and digestion.

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:01.620
If you're very riled up,

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:04.590
you could have decreased
activation of this vagus nerve,

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:06.840
and as a result, be very on edge.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:08.070
So when you take that deep breath,

00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:11.340
it actually helps normalize
the parasympathetic tone,

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:13.027
helps calm you down, and you chill out.

00:06:13.027 --> 00:06:15.453
"Why do I sometimes like
the smell of my own farts?"

00:06:15.453 --> 00:06:17.850
Okay Krissy, didn't expect
that you'd go there.

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:19.860
But there's actually a
psychological phenomenon

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:20.693
that's occurring here.

00:06:20.693 --> 00:06:24.630
The longer your body is
exposed to a specific stimuli

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:25.770
from outside the body,

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:28.290
so for example, when
you try and go to sleep,

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:29.760
white noise works well,

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:32.490
because your body starts to
tune out the white noise,

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:34.260
'cause it's the same level of noise.

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:36.720
That's why noise that's
quiet and then really loud

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:37.770
will wake you up at night,

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:39.720
'cause it triggers your
brain to react to it.

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:42.870
But if you constantly
over time smell your farts

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:45.570
and smell your own body,
you become accustomed to it.

00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:47.490
So it doesn't trigger that same level

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:48.840
of response from your brain.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:53.250
So when others smell other
farts, poop or what have you,

00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:55.830
it triggers in them that
there's a foreign substance,

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:57.390
potentially poisonous substance,

00:06:57.390 --> 00:06:58.560
that they should stay away from.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:06:59.760
But when it's your own,

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:01.590
your body's not having that threat,

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:03.330
because it's been smelling it for so long,

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:05.167
you have a decreased stimulus response.

00:07:05.167 --> 00:07:07.500
"When people train to hold
more air in the lungs,

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:09.090
what exactly is getting stronger?

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:10.350
Do the lungs inflate more?"

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:12.030
Well yeah, the lungs can inflate more,

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:14.027
hormonally, things change,

00:07:14.027 --> 00:07:18.030
cellularly, things change,

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:20.650
like you're able to produce
more red blood cells,

00:07:20.650 --> 00:07:22.530
you have a higher hematocrit,

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:27.360
you're able to withstand
periods of anaerobic stress

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:28.530
for longer periods of time,

00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:31.530
so you have better lactic
acid removal from your body.

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:34.290
So it's not just like hold
more air in your lungs,

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:38.040
it's the processing of
being in an anaerobic state

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:39.967
that is equally important
in this situation.

00:07:39.967 --> 00:07:42.810
"Hey Dr. Mike, my family tells
me that eating too much salt

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:43.890
will melt my bones.

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:44.723
Is this true?

00:07:44.723 --> 00:07:46.710
And if so, how much salt is too much?

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:48.300
Melt your bones?

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:49.710
Melt? Melt?

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:50.543
I don't know.

00:07:50.543 --> 00:07:52.140
Is it ideal to eat too much salt?

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:52.973
No.

00:07:52.973 --> 00:07:54.690
There's recommended dietary intake

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:56.490
for every age group and it's different,

00:07:56.490 --> 00:07:58.080
it's also based on health conditions,

00:07:58.080 --> 00:07:59.730
so I can't give you an exact number

00:07:59.730 --> 00:08:01.440
but just don't over salt your foods.

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:02.767
Moderation.

00:08:02.767 --> 00:08:06.030
"Why are some people able
to live well into the 90s

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:07.380
if they are heavy smokers?"

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:08.430
Well, this is important,

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:10.050
and it kind of brings up the notion

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:12.870
of what happens when you
have unhealthy habits.

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:15.060
Too often I tell patients,
"Hey, you shouldn't smoke."

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:18.660
And they say, "Well, my
loved one lived a long life

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:20.400
and didn't even have lung cancer."

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:21.780
But the important thing to remember

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:24.480
is not all smokers end
up getting lung cancer.

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:26.430
In fact, most smokers
don't get lung cancer.

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:30.660
A lot of smokers get diseases
like COPD, emphysema,

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:32.569
these are quality of life reducers

00:08:32.569 --> 00:08:35.130
and quantity of life reducers.

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:37.020
So you may live into your 90s,

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:38.640
but you have to live with an oxygen tank,

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:40.800
and have such bad dyspnea
or shortness of breath

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:42.300
that you can't walk to the bathroom

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:44.910
without feeling a tremendous
amount of air hunger.

00:08:44.910 --> 00:08:48.120
It's not just do you die,
it's how well you live,

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:50.460
and with what conditions you live.

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:53.130
I misdraw my life videos, so I made one.

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:55.650
Animated story of how I came to America.

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:56.483
Such a good,

00:08:56.483 --> 00:08:57.990
"well, I bet you don't
know a lot about me."

00:08:57.990 --> 00:08:59.943
As always, stay happy and healthy.

